 ## Background
-Local Ambient Occlusion for Volume Rendering originated from the need to improve the visibility of internal structures in medical volume rendering without the high cost of global illumination. Hernell et al. introduced the foundational Volumetric Local Ambient Occlusion (LAO) model in 2007, which used a multiresolution framework and ray casting to compute local occlusion [S1]. Later, Jönsson et al. expanded on this with interactive volume illumination techniques [S2]. A significant advancement was made in 2019 by researchers who proposed a high-speed method using local vicinity statistics and a probabilistic approach, allowing for real-time rendering with hand-drawn transfer functions by reducing the computational complexity from the data range to the number of TF segments [S3].
+Local Ambient Occlusion for Volume Rendering originated from the need to improve the visibility of internal structures in medical volume rendering without the high cost of global illumination [S1, S3]. Hernell et al. introduced the foundational Volumetric Local Ambient Occlusion (LAO) model in 2007, which used a multiresolution framework and ray casting to compute local occlusion [S1]. Later, Jönsson et al. expanded on this with interactive volume illumination techniques [S2]. A significant advancement was made in 2019 by researchers who proposed a high-speed method using local vicinity statistics and a probabilistic approach, allowing for real-time rendering with hand-drawn transfer functions by reducing the computational complexity from the data range to the number of TF segments [S3].
 
